Back to Articles Decision Models in Hugging Face Published November 10, 2026 Update on GitHub Upvote 67 +61 Aritra Roy Gosthipaty ariG23498 Follow Lysandre lysandre Follow Pedro Cuenca pcuenq Follow Tom Aarsen tomaarsen Follow Large Language Models (LLMs) are exceptionally good at generating text. We use them to answer questions, write code, reason about problems, and so much more. But not every problem requires generating text. Consider a customer support system receiving the following message: "I was charged twice for my subscription. I need a refund today." The system needs to determine which team should handle the request, how urgent it is, and whether the customer is requesting a refund. We could ask an LLM to return a JSON object containing these answers. llama cli -hf ggml-org/gemma-4-e4b-it-GGUF:Q4_0 > We have the following customer support ticket, "I was charged twice for my subscription. I need a refund today." , which team should handle the request, how urgent it is, and whether the customer is requesting a refund. Give the answers in JSON. [Start thinking]
...
[end thinking]
```json
{ "team_to_handle" : "Billing" , "urgency" : "High" , "request_refund" : true } But what if the model could directly evaluate the available options and return a probability distribution over them, without generating any text ? from transformers import pipeline decide = pipeline( "decision" , model= "autotrust/GEV-26B-Decide" )
decision = decide( { "state" : "I was charged twice for my order and want a refund." , "questions" : { "department" : { "type" : "choice" , "instructions" : "Which team should handle this?" , "criteria" : { "billing" : "Payments and refunds" , "technical" : "Bugs and technical issues" , "sales" : "Pricing and subscriptions" } }, } }
) # { # 'department': { # 'type': 'choice', # 'choice': 'billing', # 'confidence': 0.9974000453948975, # 'probabilities': { # 'billing': 0.9982666969299316, # 'technical': 0.0010246571619063616, # 'sales': 0.0007087149424478412 # } # } # } This is the key idea behind decision models . What are decision models? A decision model takes some input context (state), evaluates a question with a set of predefined possible answers (primitives), and returns a structured decision along with probabilities. Instead of asking a model to generate an answer token by token, we ask it to assign probabilities to the answers that our application already understands. TypeSafe AI introduced Jev , describing it as a System One model . The name draws inspiration from the distinction between fast, intuitive thinking (System 1) and slower, deliberative thinking (System 2). The community has converged to the term decision model , because it describes the model's computational role rather than invoking a particular theory of cognition. In the rest of the post we will refer to the models as "decision models" instead of "system one models". ( A poll on twitter deciding what we should be calling such models ) For a decision model, the fundamental interface is: State + Questions + Allowed Answers → Decisions + Probabilities Why not use LLMs for everything? We could simply prompt an LLM to return a JSON object containing our decisions. In fact, this is how many applications already use language models for classification, routing, and other structured tasks. However, there are a few problems with this approach. LLMs are not guaranteed to follow the requested schema: Even with explicit instructions, a model might produce invalid JSON, introduce unexpected keys, or return an answer outside the allowed choices. Constrained decoding helps, but does not eliminate the computational overhead: Libraries such as Outlines and XGrammar can enforce grammars or JSON schemas by restricting which tokens the model is allowed to generate at each step. This ensures the output follows the supported structural constraints, but introduces additional work. Why generate an entire response: LLMs are designed for open-ended generation, but when our application only needs to choose between predefined answers, autoregressive decoding can be unnecessary work. How are decision models different from classification models? Before understanding the interface of a decision model, it is useful to revisit two closely related approaches namely traditional classification and zero-shot classification. Traditional classification Suppose we train a classifier that routes customer-support requests to three departments: Billing Technical support Sales A conventional classification model learns a mapping from an input to a predefined set of labels. During training, the model learns to predict these labels from examples. A classification head typically produces one logit per class, and softmax turns those logits into a probability distribution. The limitation here is that the output classes are baked into the model's classification head . If we introduce a fourth department, such as Legal, the existing classifier does not automatically acquire a corresponding output class. We may need to modify the head, collect training data, and retrain or adapt the model. Zero-shot classification Zero-shot classification addresses an important part of this limitation. Instead of requiring the classifier to be trained specifically on every output category, we supply the candidate labels at inference time. For example: from transformers import pipeline classifier = pipeline( "zero-shot-classification" , model= "facebook/bart-large-mnli" ,
) classifier( "My payment was deducted twice." , candidate_labels=[ "billing" , "technical support" , "sales" ],
) # {'sequence': 'My payment was deducted twice.', # 'labels': ['billing', 'sales', 'technical support'], # 'scores': [0.8466812372207642, 0.0962134450674057, 0.05710534751415253]} Unlike a conventional fixed-label classifier, the categories can change from one request to another. Let’s add another department, such as “Legal”, and see the model outputs. from transformers import pipeline classifier = pipeline( "zero-shot-classification" , model= "facebook/bart-large-mnli" ,
) classifier( "My payment was deducted twice." , candidate_labels=[ "billing" , "technical support" , "sales" , "legal" ],
) # {'sequence': 'My payment was deducted twice.', # 'labels': ['billing', 'sales', 'technical support', 'legal'], # 'scores': [0.8185835480690002, 0.09302053600549698, 0.05521026626229286, 0.03318570926785469]} One common implementation uses a model trained for natural language inference (NLI). Each candidate label is converted into a hypothesis, and the model measures how strongly the input entails that hypothesis. This already sounds quite similar to a decision model. And in many ways, it is. Where decision models differ Decision models extend this idea beyond simply assigning an input to a category. A decision model can accept several named questions about the same state, with different answer types. The types can be choosing between alternatives (choice), scoring an ordered scale (score), or estimating the probability that a statement is true (noul). More importantly, its interface is designed around the probability distribution over possible decisions . Consider these three approaches: Property Classification Zero-shot classification Decision model Probability over choices Yes Yes Yes Labels defined at inference No Yes Yes Multiple typed questions No No Yes Ordered scoring and yes/no decisions Task-specific Possible with adaptation Native primitives The advantage of decision models is not that they invented runtime-defined labels or probabilistic classification. It is that they organize these capabilities into a consistent interface, potentially backed by architectures and training procedures optimized specifically for decisions. The decision model interface A decision model request has the following two components: state questions State The state is the information the model should evaluate.

Integrating LLM Translation into Your Scikit-Learn Pipelines with Scikit-LLM Learn, in a few easy steps, how to build a two-stage scikit-learn pipeline for multilingual text classification, guided by two pre-trained language models handled by Scikit-LLM for translation and zero-shot classification. By Iván Palomares Carrascosa , KDnuggets Technical Content Specialist on November 10, 2026 in Language Models Introduction Text classification sometimes requires dealing with raw texts written in different languages, with the added challenge of normalizing all incoming data into a unified language before providing it to the downstream classification model. Thankfully, Scikit-LLM not only facilitates the construction of pipelines driven by large language models (LLMs) for tasks like zero-shot and few-shot classification . It also provides custom classes to adapt pre-trained models for other specialized language tasks like translation . This article shows how to build an overarching scikit-learn pipeline for multilingual text classification , consisting of two major stages, both guided by Scikit-LLM-wrapped models: translation and zero-shot classification. The resulting pipeline I'll teach you to build has multiple advantages: It replaces the rigid keyword matching of traditional text classifiers with semantic reasoning, thanks to a pre-trained LLM wrapped for zero-shot classification Incoming texts are unified into a common language to enable effective classification with a single downstream model Zero-shot classification with Scikit-LLM addresses the cold-start data issue, needing only category names (class labels) along with a few associated training examples to start doing its job: no extra model training needed at all! Building a Multilingual Translation and Zero-Shot Classification Pipeline This example can be run entirely in a Google Colab notebook. It uses Groq 's LLM API to rely entirely on free models. As a prerequisite, go to the Groq website , register, and generate your own API key. Once it is generated, carefully copy your API key (it is shown only once) and paste it as a new "Secret" variable in Google Colab, found under the key icon on the left-hand side of Colab. Here, you'll paste your copied API key and name the secret variable GROQ_API_KEY . After that, we start our notebook by installing Scikit-LLM: !pip install scikit-llm -q Now, let's start writing our Python code. The first step is to configure the environment surrounding Scikit-LLM, routing our requests through Groq's API instead of the default OpenAI API. By doing this, we leverage Groq's free, user-friendly pre-trained models, avoiding per-token costs. from google.colab import userdata from skllm.config import SKLLMConfig # Retrieving API key and route to a free-tier OpenAI-compatible endpoint (e.g. Groq) groq_key = userdata.get('GROQ_API_KEY') SKLLMConfig.set_gpt_url("https://api.groq.com/openai/v1") SKLLMConfig.set_openai_key(groq_key) Next, we define the "training" data . Since we are going to use a zero-shot LLM classifier — instead of a traditional machine learning classifier preceded by a vectorizer that maps text numerically — we have the advantage of not needing a large, balanced set of training examples. All we need is to define the unique set of classification labels that the model must learn to recognize and use for predictions, as well as a few placeholder data examples that fill the minimal requirements of Scikit-LLM's fit() method, which mimics its scikit-learn counterpart: # 1. MULTILINGUAL TRAINING DATA # Adding a few labeled examples in mixed languages and multiple topics X_multilingual_texts = [ # Energy "La technologie des panneaux solaires a vu son efficacité augmenter de 15%.", # French (Solar) "Neue Batteriespeicher für Windenergie werden immer wirtschaftlicher.", # German (Wind/Battery) # Finance "Los mercados financieros sufrieron una fuerte caída hoy tras la subida de tipos.", # Spanish (Markets) "Les actions technologiques ont rebondi grâce à de solides rapports sur les bénéfices.", # French (Stocks/Earnings) # Healthcare "Die klinische Studie für das neue Medikament reduzierte die Schwellung um 40%.", # German (Medication/Trial) "Un ensayo reciente demostró que la vacuna es muy eficaz para prevenir infecciones.", # Spanish (Vaccine) ] y_labels = ["Energy", "Energy", "Finance", "Finance", "Healthcare", "Healthcare"] The next step is the central one: we define our two-stage pipeline for translation followed by zero-shot classification . This is where we integrate Scikit-LLM's capabilities for adapting pre-trained LLMs to specific tasks into a broader, scikit-learn-based machine learning pipeline. The code below uses two task-specific classes from Scikit-LLM: GPTTranslator to adapt a base LLM for translation into a common language, and ZeroShotGPTClassifier to adapt another LLM for the subsequent task in our pipeline, i.e. classification of the translated text without the need to retrain the model. Notice below that it is perfectly fine to choose the same base Groq model — "custom_url::llama-3.1-8b-instant" — for both stages: we just use different classes to adapt the two model instances to different target tasks. from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline from skllm.models.gpt.text2text.translation import GPTTranslator from skllm.models.gpt.classification.zero_shot import ZeroShotGPTClassifier # 2. THE TRANSLATION AND ZERO-SHOT CLASSIFICATION PIPELINE # A scikit-learn-based pipeline that contains two Scikit-LLM-wrapped models text_pipeline = Pipeline([ ('translator', GPTTranslator( model="custom_url::openai/gpt-oss-20b", output_language="English" )), ('classifier', ZeroShotGPTClassifier( model="custom_url::openai/gpt-oss-20b" )) ]) # 3. FITTING THE CLASSIFIER # ZeroShotGPTClassifier only needs to see the unique labels to understand the categories. # Passing a minimal placeholder list for X only to satisfy the scikit-learn API requirements. y_labels_unique = ["Energy", "Finance", "Healthcare"] X_placeholder = [""] * len(y_labels_unique) text_pipeline.fit(X_placeholder, y_labels_unique) Before making a few final predictions by passing fresh examples to our newly built pipeline, we take a look at the translations produced by the first of the two models for the training examples to verify that they are correct: # Accessing the 'translator' step to transform the training data train_translations = text_pipeline.named_steps['translator'].transform(X_multilingual_texts) print("--- TRAINING DATA TRANSLATIONS ---") for label, original, translated in zip(y_labels, X_multilingual_texts, train_translations): print(f"[{label.upper()}]
Original: {original}
Translated: {translated}
") Output: --- TRAINING DATA TRANSLATIONS --- [ENERGY] Original: La technologie des panneaux solaires a vu son efficacité augmenter de 15%. Translated: The technology of solar panels has seen its efficiency increase by 15%. [ENERGY] Original: Neue Batteriespeicher für Windenergie werden immer wirtschaftlicher. Translated: New battery storage for wind energy is becoming increasingly economical. [FINANCE] Original: Los mercados financieros sufrieron una fuerte caída hoy tras la subida de tipos. Translated: Financial markets suffered a sharp drop today after the rate hike. [FINANCE] Original: Les actions technologiques ont rebondi grâce à de solides rapports sur les bénéfices. Translated: Tech stocks rebounded thanks to solid earnings reports. [HEALTHCARE] Original: Die klinische Studie für das neue Medikament reduzierte die Schwellung um 40%. Translated: The clinical study for the new drug reduced swelling by 40%. [HEALTHCARE] Original: Un ensayo reciente demostró que la vacuna es muy eficaz para prevenir infecciones. Translated: A recent study showed that the vaccine is very effective at preventing infections.
